The framebuffer the console binds to has to be marked primary in time

The text console binds to the framebuffer the vendor driver marks
FBINFO_PRIMARY. The DRM's fbcon helper reads that flag when the
framebuffer is REGISTERED, and the registration happens inside
am_meson_create_drm_fbdev() - which is a few lines above where the
flag used to be set. So the flag was always set after the framebuffer
carrying it had been registered and judged, and the console never
bound to it.

It worked by luck of ordering: while the vendor's OSD overlays
registered first, the DRM's framebuffer registered later, by which time
the flag had been set. Once the overlays stopped registering - the
framebuffer is then the only one, and is registered synchronously - the
flag was always too late and the console stopped taking the display
over.

So the flag is set in the driver's fb_probe callback, which is where
the fb_info is created and which runs before the registration, in every
ordering. The old assignment is removed, so that there is one place
that sets it and it is the place that is early enough.
diff --git a/drivers/amlogic/drm/meson_fbdev.c b/drivers/amlogic/drm/meson_fbdev.c
--- a/drivers/amlogic/drm/meson_fbdev.c
+++ b/drivers/amlogic/drm/meson_fbdev.c
@@ -726,6 +726,36 @@
 	fbi->screen_size = fb->pitches[0] * fb->height;
 	fbi->fix.smem_len = fbi->screen_size;
 
+	/*
+	 * This is the framebuffer that is scanned out to the display, so
+	 * it is the one the text console belongs to. Say so explicitly:
+	 * without it the console binds to whichever framebuffer
+	 * registered first, and the vendor's OSD overlays are not
+	 * CPU-accessible - the console's blitter would write to an
+	 * unmapped address and the machine would oops.
+	 *
+	 * IT HAS TO BE SET HERE, in the probe, and not in
+	 * am_meson_drm_fbdev_init(). The probe is what creates this
+	 * fb_info, and drm_fb_helper_probe_complete() calls
+	 * register_framebuffer() on it as soon as the probe returns.
+	 * register_framebuffer() is what calls fbcon_fb_registered(),
+	 * and that is where FBINFO_PRIMARY is read. So a flag set
+	 * after this function returns is too late: the framebuffer has
+	 * already been registered, and judged, without it.
+	 *
+	 * That is not a theoretical problem. It is what broke the
+	 * console: the flag used to be set in the init, a few
+	 * instructions after am_meson_create_drm_fbdev() - which
+	 * registers the framebuffer - returned. It happened to work
+	 * while the OSD overlays registered first, because the DRM's
+	 * framebuffer was then registered later, by which time the
+	 * flag was set. Once the overlays stopped registering (the
+	 * framebuffer is then the only one, and is registered
+	 * synchronously), the flag was always too late and the console
+	 * stopped taking over the display.
+	 */
+	fbi->flags |= FBINFO_PRIMARY;
+
 	drm_fb_helper_fill_info(fbi, helper, &sizes);
 	am_meson_drm_fbdev_modeset_create(helper);
 
@@ -900,14 +930,12 @@
 		fbdev = am_meson_create_drm_fbdev(dev, drmdev->primary_plane);
 		fbdev->zorder = OSD_PLANE_BEGIN_ZORDER;
 		/*
-		 * This is the framebuffer that is scanned out to the display,
-		 * so it is the one the text console belongs to. Say so
-		 * explicitly: without it the console binds to whichever
-		 * framebuffer registered first, which is one of the OSD
-		 * overlays, and those are not CPU-accessible.
+		 * The framebuffer is marked FBINFO_PRIMARY in
+		 * am_meson_drm_fbdev_probe(), which is the only place it
+		 * can be marked in time: the framebuffer is registered
+		 * inside am_meson_create_drm_fbdev(), and the console
+		 * reads the flag when it is registered.
 		 */
-		if (fbdev->base.fbdev)
-			fbdev->base.fbdev->flags |= FBINFO_PRIMARY;
 		DRM_INFO("create fbdev for primary plane [%p]\n", fbdev);
 	}
 
